Twisted Mirror Path Count
LeetCode Problem
Approach: Recursion + Memoization (Dynamic Programming)
Intuition
We use recursion with memoization to count all valid paths from the top-left corner to the bottom-right corner.

At every cell, we maintain a boolean variable right to represent the direction in which the robot entered the current cell:
- true: Robot entered while moving right.
- false: Robot entered while moving down.
There are two possible types of cells:
1. Empty cell (0): The robot can move either right or down.
2. Mirror cell (1): The robot gets reflected depending on its incoming direction:
   - Entering while moving right redirects the robot down.
   - Entering while moving down redirects the robot right.
We use a 3D DP array:

dp[row][col][right]

This stores the number of valid paths from a particular cell with a given incoming direction.
C++ Code  

class Solution {
public:
    const int MOD = 1e9 + 7;
    int dp[500][500][2];

    int solve(vector<vector<int>>& grid, int row, int col, bool right) {
        if(row < 0 || col < 0 || row >= grid.size() || col >= grid[0].size())
            return 0;

        if(row == grid.size()-1 && col == grid[0].size()-1)
            return 1;

        if(dp[row][col][right] != -1)
            return dp[row][col][right];

        long long ans = 0;

        if(grid[row][col] == 1) {
            if(right)
                ans += solve(grid,row+1,col,false);
            else
                ans += solve(grid,row,col+1,true);
        }
        else {
            ans += solve(grid,row,col+1,true);
            ans += solve(grid,row+1,col,false);
        }

        return dp[row][col][right] = ans % MOD;
    }

    int uniquePaths(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {
        memset(dp,-1,sizeof(dp));

        if(grid[0][0] == 0)
            return solve(grid,0,0,true);

        return (solve(grid,0,0,true) + solve(grid,0,0,false)) % MOD;
    }
};

Complexity Analysis
Let m be the number of rows and n be the number of columns.
- Time Complexity: \(O(m \times n)\)
  
  Each cell has two possible direction states, and each state is calculated only once.
- Space Complexity: \(O(m \times n)\)
  
  The DP array requires \(O(m \times n \times 2)\) space, along with recursion stack space.
Important Observations
- Direction is an important part of the DP state because mirror behavior depends on the incoming direction.
- We don't need a visited array because the robot can only move right or down, so cycles are impossible.
- Memoization avoids recalculating the same cell and direction combination.
- Modulo is applied to prevent the answer from becoming too large.
